# SCOPE NOTE (read this first)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This script tests statistical properties of the RIEMANN ZETA ZERO SEQUENCE
# ITSELF, against three families of theoretical predictions:
# (1) Convergence to Gram points [b(N) statistic — Objection 1]
# (2) Nearest-neighbour spacing statistics [GUE / Wigner–Dyson — Objection 2]
# (3) Large-T decay rate of b(N) [log-log slope ≈ −0.5 — Objection 3]
# (4) Long-range spectral statistics [Σ²(L), Δ₃(L) — Objection 2 extended]

# In an earlier version, this script did NOT construct or diagonalize the
# physical AB-Cloud Hofstadter lattice Hamiltonian — it only tested the
# zeta-zero sequence. The AB-cloud Hamiltonian (v23 §1.3, eq. 1.4) IS now
# implemented (section 6b), along with the 3D PSL(2,7) Klein quartic
# embedding (section 6c), and Tests 15-22 directly verify the AB-cloud
# model claims. Where the earlier code says "matrix size" or "reference
# ensemble size", it means the size of a genuine random GUE Hermitian
# matrix built on the fly (via LinearAlgebra stdlib) purely as a
# statistical reference/control, not a physical lattice.
# =============================================================================
# Usage
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# julia ab_cloud_v20.jl # interactive
# julia ab_cloud_v20.jl --zeros 50000 --test all
# julia ab_cloud_v20.jl --zeros 50000 --source embedded
# julia ab_cloud_v20.jl --zeros 200000 --source 2M --test all
# julia ab_cloud_v20.jl --zeros 20000 --matrix-size 3000 --test all
# julia ab_cloud_v20.jl --config # interactive config
# julia ab_cloud_v20.jl --help
# =============================================================================
# Dependencies: Julia ≥ 1.6, standard library only (Printf, LinearAlgebra,
# Random, Statistics, Dates). Optional: Plots.jl for PNG output
# (ASCII fallback is always available).
# =============================================================================

import Printf: @printf, @sprintf
import LinearAlgebra: norm, eigvals, Hermitian, I, Diagonal, eigen, Symmetric, det, rmul!, mul!, dot # dot = FHS link product (Test 20); mul!/rmul! in-place ops for Test 27
import Random: seed!, randperm, rand, randn, randn!, rand!, default_rng, MersenneTwister # rand! for the hardcore bootstrap buffer; randn! fills the lean GUE matrix buffer in place
import Statistics: mean, std, median, quantile, cor, var
import Dates

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 1. CONFIGURATION AND BILINGUAL MESSAGES
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

"""
 Config

All tunable parameters of the verification suite, collected into one mutable
struct so that the interactive configuration menu and the CLI parser can both
modify the same state. Fields are grouped by purpose:

**Data selection**
- `zeros_count` — how many zeros to actually load (capped by source capacity)
- `source` — `"embedded"` | `"50k"` | `"500k"` | `"2M"` | `"highT"` | `"zeros6"` | `"csv"` | `"auto"`
- `data_dir` — directory containing external data files
- `lang` — `"en"` | `"ru"` (UI/output language)

**Test orchestration**
- `test` — `"interactive"` | `"all"` | `"1".."14"` | `"config_then_interactive"`
- `output_dir` — where to write the report and PNG plots
- `plot` — generate Plots.jl PNGs (falls back to ASCII if Plots.jl missing)
- `verbose` — print extra diagnostics

**Original-test parameters**
- `n_checkpoints` — number of convergence-table checkpoints (0 = auto)
- `n_monotone` — number of monotonicity checks (Test 2)
- `n_bootstrap` — number of bootstrap resamples (Test 9)
- `chi2_bins` — number of χ² histogram bins (Test 6)
- `highT_mins` — threshold T values for Test 5
- `stability_fraction` — subsample fraction for Test 10
- `stability_threshold` — PASS threshold for Test 10
- `bN_pass_threshold` — PASS threshold for b(N)
- `power_law_tolerance` — tolerance for the power-law exponent (Test 3)
- `slope_ci_tolerance` — tolerance for the decay-slope CI (Test 7)

**Advanced RMT parameters**
- `gue_matrix_size` — size N of the simulated reference GUE matrix
- `gue_matrix_seed` — RNG seed for the reference matrix
- `ad_sample_cap` — cap on #spacings used for Anderson–Darling
 (default 50_000 = the FULL embedded ζ-set)
- `t31_lmax` — max lattice L for the TEST 30 Dirac-gap 1/L fit (default 72,
  CLI `--t31-lmax`; grid is all L ≡ 0 (mod 4) up to lmax drawn from
  [8,12,16,20,24,28,32,36,40,48,64,72,80,96]; exact
 dense diagonalization is O(L⁶) — L=96 already costs minutes, "L=20000"
 (4·10⁸ sites) is beyond any exact-diagonalization method)
- `rmt_cdf_table` — interpolation table for the β=2 surmise CDF (serves
 CLI `--rmt-table off` to disable; 32 768 points on [0,5], |ΔF| ≤ 1e-9,
 makes test 11's ~10⁸ CDF calls ~30× cheaper)
- `rigidity_L_values` — L grid for Σ²(L) / Δ₃(L) (TESTS 13, 14).
 **Fully configurable** via three channels:
 (1) CLI: `--L-values "2,5,10,20,50,100,140"`
 `--L-max 140 [--L-count 8]` (geometric grid)
 (2) Menu: press `c` → item 18 (explicit list)
 → item 19 (auto-grid by L_max)
 (3) Code: edit `default_config()` below.
 Default: `[2.0, 5.0, 10.0, 20.0, 50.0]`.
- `rigidity_n_windows` — window placements for Σ²(L)
- `rigidity_n_windows_d3` — window placements for Δ₃(L) (costlier)
- `rigidity_n_grid` — grid points per window for Δ₃(L)
"""
mutable struct Config
 zeros_count::Int
 source::String
 data_dir::String
 lang::String
 test::String
 output_dir::String
 plot::Bool
 verbose::Bool
 # ── Original test parameters ──
 n_checkpoints::Int
 n_monotone::Int
 n_bootstrap::Int
 chi2_bins::Int
